# Database & Custom Tables (ADR-005, ADR-018, ADR-031)

ADR-005 established the general principle that operational data lives in
custom indexed tables, never postmeta — it did **not** define or retain the
retired session-wide order-capacity mechanism (that was ADR-006, formally
superseded by ADR-028/BR-041). See "Electronic Card Limit" below for the
current model.

## Source of Truth Split

- Commerce data (products, orders, customers, taxes): WooCommerce + HPOS,
  per-site table sets (native Multisite isolation). BingoCommerce v1 does not
  support shipping (BR-048) — do not build shipping-rate, shipping-label, or
  carrier-integration logic.
- Identity: network-global via WordPress table abstractions — use
  `$wpdb->users` / `$wpdb->usermeta` in application code (shared login across
  halls). Do not hardcode literal `wp_users`/`wp_usermeta` table names; this
  installation's configured prefix currently resolves them to
  `bc_users`/`bc_usermeta` (ADR-018).
- Bingo Catalog domain data — Game Types, Bingo Play Product metadata,
  Package/Package-Component definitions, add-on eligibility (BR-037/BR-039/
  BR-040/BR-061/BR-062/BR-071/BR-072, ADR-026/ADR-031/ADR-035): within the
  single authoritative Bingo Innovation Portal environment, Portal owns Game
  Type identity/configuration by `game_types.id`, while the approved
  per-site Game Type table becomes its hall-scoped local replica. BingoCommerce
  remains authoritative for Play Product and Package structured domain data.
  Runtime logic must never infer them from WooCommerce titles, SKUs, tags, categories,
  naming conventions, or BR-032 projection metadata. Linked WooCommerce
  products are commerce shells; their IDs are unique mappings, not domain IDs.
- Portal owns operational Session-template identity. BingoCommerce owns the
  separate commerce schedule, structured commerce time, dated occurrences,
  30-day sales window, current-definition membership, Available for Sale state,
  Package-to-occurrence assignment, and the optional hall electronic-limit
  override. ADR-031's two baseline Session tables remain in place; Session
  schema v3 adds the Portal-template replica and three schedule-definition
  tables without replacing them. ADR-043 is approved and implemented;
  production deployment is not claimed. The
  platform electronic maximum is a
  network option; the optional hall override is a site option;
  `bingocommerce-catalog` resolves the effective current value. Do not store
  these in unindexed postmeta.
- Portal Hall identity is `halls.id`, and Portal Session-template identity is
  `bingo_sessions.id`. Only Sessions satisfying
  `bingo_sessions.hall_id == mapped halls.id` are Commerce v1 sync eligible;
  received/shared Sessions and `allSessions()` semantics are excluded. This is
  an authority rule, not a schema or query design.
- Structured Commerce time is BingoCommerce-owned and may be entered manually
  by a Hall Manager in v1. Time must never be parsed from display names. One
  Package may be assigned to multiple dated commerce occurrences.
- Bingo operational data already governed by existing ADRs (digital
  tickets/entitlement snapshots, redemption events, hall billing status,
  audit log per ADR-023, reporting facts) must not be dumped into unindexed
  postmeta merely for convenience. ADR-031 defines a seven-table conceptual
  baseline. The current repository implements all seven baseline tables
  plus the additive Portal-template and three Commerce-schedule tables: eleven
  tables total. ADR-044 Slice 1 implements Catalog v6's `bingo_package_addons`
  migration and schema verification only. `PackageAddonService`, the fixed
  mutation profile, UI, projection and add-on audit remain unimplemented.
  Operational domains still explicitly deferred — including final entitlement,
  redemption accumulation, device, and offline persistence — MUST NOT have
  storage invented ahead of their dedicated designs. Where an existing ADR
  already requires an operational custom indexed table, follow that ADR without
  inferring its undecided schema from ADR-031.
- A future Android/tablet device registry is not yet decided: its existence,
  persistence, ownership scope, and schema are deferred to the upcoming
  Android/API persistence design. Do not classify it as per-site or
  network-wide ahead of that design, and do not introduce a device-table
  name or schema here. The only currently locked concept is that
  `caller_id` is the tablet identity for redemption purposes (BR-045).
  Storage scope, when decided, follows ADR-022 ownership rules. Do not
  require a device registry in the current implementation.

## Table Rules

- Per-site tables: `$wpdb->prefix . '<name>'` (auto-scoped to the hall).
- Network-wide tables: `$wpdb->base_prefix . '<name>'`. Tenant-attributed
  network records require an indexed `blog_id`/`site_id` column (ADR-022);
  truly network-global records/configuration (e.g. the platform electronic
  maximum) must not invent a fake hall/site ID when no tenant attribution
  exists.
- Tables that do have a hall/site reference column must index it. Other
  common indexed columns as applicable: `session_id`, `order_id`, `status`,
  `created_at`, `expires_at`, `token_hash`. Redemption/device-identity column
  naming is not locked here — BR-045's `caller_id` is the confirmed Android
  tablet-identity concept, but exact schema belongs to the technical-design
  pass.
- Migration versioning is plugin-owned, not per-table (see
  `docs/architecture/plugin-architecture.md` §4 Migration Framework) — do not
  invent a separate per-table version option unless a later ADR explicitly
  decides that. Version state scope follows the artifact's own scope:
  per-site schema/artifacts use a per-site version option (e.g.
  `bc_<plugin>_db_version`); network-wide schema/artifacts use a
  network-scope version option (e.g. `bc_core_network_db_version`, per
  ADR-025 item 8) — do not assume every plugin/table schema version is
  necessarily per-site. Create/update via `dbDelta()` as the required default;
  an explicit ADR may authorize an operation `dbDelta()` cannot perform.
  The current sole exception is ADR-039's removal of `name_unique` from the
  trusted Hall-local `bingo_game_types` table. It must use the fixed allowlisted
  table suffix `bingo_game_types` and index name `name_unique`, verify the exact
  index before removal, be idempotent, and verify the final schema afterward.
  The schema version must not advance until verification succeeds. This does
  not permit general-purpose or user-controlled structural SQL. Migrations must be
  idempotent, backward-aware, and safe to run at network scale, for both
  existing sites and newly created Multisite sites.
- Table names must be distinctive — never reuse WP core or WooCommerce table
  names (ADR-018).

## Catalog and Session Tables

ADR-031's seven approved conceptual baseline tables are:

- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_game_types'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_play_products'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_packages'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_package_components'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_package_addons'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_sessions'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_session_packages'`

Of that baseline, the current Catalog v6 and Session v3 schemas implement every
listed table. ADR-044 Slice 1 implements only the add-on migration/schema
verifier; its service and other behavior remain pending. The current
repository's Session schema v3 also contains these hall-local tables under
approved ADR-043:

- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_portal_session_templates'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_session_schedules'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_session_schedule_dates'`
- `$wpdb->prefix . 'bingo_session_schedule_packages'`

Do not add another `bc_` sub-prefix or a redundant `blog_id`. The per-site
table prefix is the tenant boundary. Game Type, Bingo Play Product, Package,
and Session use local surrogate domain IDs. Portal `game_types.id` is the
trusted cross-system Game Type identity, and Portal `bingo_sessions.id` is the
external operational template identity represented by the hall-local replica. A linked
WooCommerce product ID must be unique within its mapping and must not be
registered simultaneously as both a Package and Bingo Play Product.
Enforcement MUST be concurrency-safe; a
standalone check-then-insert across the two authoritative tables is
insufficient.

Ordinary `$wpdb` SQL uses `$wpdb->prepare()`. Direct mysqli is allowed ONLY
inside an approved captured-`$wpdb->dbh` transport, and every such transport
binds values through native mysqli prepared statements. The approved transports
are ADR-025 (Hall Staff mandatory audit), ADR-032 (Catalog critical
serialization, including ADR-034's approved third use for Play Product
medium-change integrity against Package-component references), ADR-033
(Package composition atomicity), ADR-044 (the not-yet-implemented fixed
Package-add-on mutation profile plus its narrow add-on integrity extensions to
ADR-033/ADR-034), and ADR-040 (Portal snapshot application).
ADR-033 additionally PROHIBITS `$wpdb->query()`
for statements issued while its transaction is open. No other feature may
introduce direct mysqli without reviewed authority. Raw value interpolation into
SQL remains prohibited everywhere.

Approved ADR-043 introduces no transaction transport or advisory-lock transport;
its schedule writes use ordinary prepared `$wpdb` statements. Do not remove or
mischaracterize the separate, valid Portal synchronization or ADR-042
provisioning locks.

These transports do NOT share one contract. Each boundary has its own permitted
statement set, lock order, commit semantics, and retry behaviour, and they
differ in ways that matter: ADR-025 REQUIRES its mandatory audit insert inside
the transaction, whereas the Catalog boundaries REQUIRE AuditLog writes to stay
outside and to run only after a confirmed commit. Always follow the ADR that
governs the boundary you are working in, and never carry a rule across from
another one.

Inside the CATALOG boundaries (ADR-032, its ADR-034 extension, ADR-033 and
ADR-044) use
the captured `$wpdb->dbh` connection, verified current-site context, and the
canonical Catalog lock order; keep WooCommerce and WordPress writes,
metadata/projection writes, AuditLog writes, hooks, callbacks, caches, emails,
and UI effects outside; fail closed when the transport or connection state is
unsafe; and do not add another table or import ADR-025 operation IDs, witnesses,
mandatory-audit atomicity, Hall Staff classes, retry tokens, or reconciliation
behavior. Consult the applicable ADR for that boundary's exact contract rather
than restating it here.

Use indexed logical relationships and restrictive canonical-service reference
validation initially. Do not assume physical foreign keys or cascading deletes.
For existing local transitional Catalog lifecycle behavior, archive preserves
relationships and hard delete is blocked while referenced; no lifecycle
operation silently detaches relationships. This does not create Portal v1 Game
Type Archive/Delete behavior. The conceptual schema is
in `docs/architecture/catalog-session-persistence-design.md`.

Under BR-065, BR-032's `bc_product_kind` and `bc_package_relation` are required
derived WooCommerce compatibility/cart-enforcement projections. Catalog tables
are authoritative. Under ADR-044/BR-079, the not-yet-implemented
`bingo_package_addons` table alone determines exact Package/add-on eligibility.
Projection writes through WooCommerce CRUD are deterministic, idempotent,
verifiable, and reconcilable. Do not create a direct-SQL transaction spanning
Catalog rows and WooCommerce metadata or assume ADR-025 applies.

ADR-044 fixes `unit_price` as canonical non-negative `DECIMAL(19,2)`, persisted,
displayed, compared for no-op, audited and fingerprinted with exactly two
fractional digits, and `max_quantity` as an integer from 1 through 99, default
1. Manager/service input must already contain exactly two fractional digits; do
not normalize `5`, `5.0` or `5.5`. Reject floating-point input, exponent form,
signs, noncanonical leading zeros, excess precision, overflow and silent
rounding. UI-only WooCommerce prefill padding does not relax the service rule.
The fixed add-on writer locks Package, Play Product, then relationship; bulk UI
saves invoke that one-row operation independently. Enabled add-on references
participate in both-direction medium integrity and Package composition
compatibility. Medium transition locks the Play Product, performs the existing
locking component-reference check, then the locking enabled-add-on-reference
check; either blocking reference returns the existing
`play_product_package_referenced` code with cause-identifying details, not a new
overlapping code. It takes no Package lock. Composition replacement locks the
Package, reads enabled add-ons without locking relationship rows ahead of their
Play Products, then locks the complete base/add-on Play Product dependency set once
in ascending ID order. Audit and projection run only after confirmed commit;
ambiguous COMMIT emits neither. Targeted projection retry accepts the current-
Hall `bingo_play_product_id` and resolves WooCommerce identity internally. The
Hall-wide mismatch scanner is deferred to the storefront/readiness phase.

## Digital Ticket Table (conceptual data requirements — exact final schema is
a later technical-design decision per ADR-029/ADR-030)

Needs to track: an unguessable ticket reference (store a hash where
practical), the order it was issued from (ONE ticket per Digital/Hybrid
order, not per line item — BR-044), `blog_id`, status (`issued` / `redeemed`
/ `void`), the redeeming device/tablet identity (BR-045's `caller_id` is the
confirmed Android tablet identity — do not lock a differently-named
device-identity column ahead of the technical-design pass), redemption time,
and reactivation history (count, last timestamp, last staff actor — BR-010).

## Electronic Card Limit (BR-038/BR-042/BR-045 — read this before touching checkout or redemption)

- There is NO session-wide qualifying-order capacity counter. That model
  (formerly ADR-006/BR-025, now superseded by ADR-028/BR-041) is retired — do
  not implement `used = used + 1 WHERE used < cap` for orders, and do not
  build a per-Session order/customer cap.
- The electronic-card limit is a configurable maximum digital FACES, evaluated
  independently per Game Type — never cumulative across Game Types. Only
  Digital Bingo Play Products contribute; Paper always contributes zero.
- Effective limit = `min(hall_override, current_platform_maximum)` if a hall
  override exists, else the current platform maximum — always evaluated
  against the platform maximum's CURRENT value, even if a hall's saved
  override predates a later platform-maximum decrease.
- The platform electronic maximum is a network option controlled by Platform
  Admin; the optional hall override is a site option configurable by an
  authorized Hall Manager. `bingocommerce-catalog` owns the resolver. Do not
  describe the platform maximum itself as per-site (BR-064/ADR-031).
- Checkout performs authoritative server-side validation against the current
  effective limit (BR-042) — it does not increment or reserve a shared
  counter, so no transactional locking is required at checkout merely
  because the retired ADR-006 model once needed it.
- Android redemption checks and mutates accumulated per-tablet/session/
  Game-Type usage (BR-045, BR-055) and MUST be atomic/concurrency-safe —
  read-then-write is a race condition and is rejected; each incoming
  Package/receipt is accepted or rejected as a whole. Multiple valid Digital
  Packages for the same Session may redeem onto the same tablet and
  accumulate independently by Game Type. Do not introduce an All Game Types
  Game Type or combined Game-Type bucket (BR-057). Do not design per-game
  play-history storage as part of this feature (BR-058).
- UI-side calculation alone is never sufficient at either boundary.
- The platform electronic maximum is configurable (currently 300). NEVER
  hardcode 300.
- Configuration persistence is locked by ADR-031. Exact table/column design for
  later per-tablet/session/Game-Type redemption accumulation remains deferred
  to its dedicated technical-design pass.

## Reporting

- NEVER loop across hall sites/tables per request for platform reports. Write
  normalized network reporting facts in the background when orders change.
- Keep purchase/payment date separate from Session attribution (BR-054).
  Example: $4,000 paid Monday for Tuesday's Session and $6,000 paid Tuesday
  for Tuesday's Session means daily transaction reporting is Monday = $4,000
  collected and Tuesday = $6,000 collected, while Session reporting is
  Tuesday Session = $10,000 total Session sales. Do not move the Monday
  transaction onto Tuesday's payment/accounting date.
